@@ category: Bit Manipulation

@@@ problem 67 add-binary
title: Add Binary
difficulty: Easy
topics: Math, String, Bit Manipulation, Simulation
params: a, b
time: O(max(m, n))
space: O(max(m, n))
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n unit=digits

=== description
Given two binary strings `a` and `b`, return their sum as a binary string.

=== constraints
- `1 <= a.length, b.length <= 10^4`
- `a` and `b` consist only of `'0'` and `'1'`
- no leading zeros except for `"0"` itself

=== hints
- Add from the least significant digit (the end of the strings), carrying like in decimal addition.
- Build the result backwards and reverse it at the end.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    string addBinary(string a, string b) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::addBinary);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto bin = [&](int len) {
            std::string s = "1" + g.str(std::max(0, len - 1), "01");
            if (g.coin(0.1)) s = "0";
            if (g.coin(0.1)) s = std::string(len, '1');
            return s;
        };
        return lc::lines(bin(std::max(n, 1)), bin(g.irange(1, std::max(n, 1))));
    });
}

=== approach
Walk both strings from the end with indices `i` and `j` and a `carry`. At each step add the available digits and the carry: the result digit is `sum % 2` and the new carry `sum / 2`. Continue while either string has digits or a carry remains. Digits are produced least-significant first, so reverse the result at the end.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    string addBinary(string a, string b) {
        string out;
        int i = (int)a.size() - 1, j = (int)b.size() - 1, carry = 0;
        while (i >= 0 || j >= 0 || carry) {
            int sum = carry;
            if (i >= 0) sum += a[i--] - '0';
            if (j >= 0) sum += b[j--] - '0';
            out.push_back('0' + sum % 2);    // current bit
            carry = sum / 2;
        }
        reverse(out.begin(), out.end());     // we built it least-significant first
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
"11"
"1"
> "100"

"1010"
"1011"
> "10101"

=== tests
"0"
"0"
> "0"

"1"
"111"
> "1000"

"1111"
"1111"
> "11110"

@@@ problem 190 reverse-bits
title: Reverse Bits
difficulty: Easy
topics: Divide and Conquer, Bit Manipulation
params: n
time: O(1)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Reverse the bits of a 32-bit unsigned integer `n`: bit 0 becomes bit 31, bit 1 becomes bit 30, and so on. Return the result.

In this judge, inputs and outputs are written as ordinary decimal integers.

Follow-up: if this function is called many times, how would you optimize it?

=== constraints
- `0 <= n <= 2^32 - 1`

=== hints
- Shift the answer left and append `n`'s lowest bit, 32 times.
- Faster: swap halves, then quarters, then bytes, … with masks (divide and conquer).

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    uint32_t reverseBits(uint32_t n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::reverseBits);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) { return lc::lines((uint32_t)g.range(0, 4294967295LL)); });
}

=== approach
Process the 32 bits one at a time: shift the result left by one, OR in the lowest bit of `n`, then shift `n` right. After 32 steps the first bit read (bit 0) has moved all the way to bit 31.

For repeated calls you could use a 256-entry table of reversed bytes, or the mask trick that swaps 16-bit halves, then 8-bit groups, 4, 2 and 1.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    uint32_t reverseBits(uint32_t n) {
        uint32_t result = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) {
            result = (result << 1) | (n & 1);   // append n's lowest bit
            n >>= 1;
        }
        return result;
    }
};

=== examples
43261596
> 964176192
: 00000010100101000001111010011100 reversed is 00111001011110000010100101000000.

4294967293
> 3221225471

=== tests
0
> 0

1
> 2147483648

4294967295
> 4294967295

2147483648
> 1

@@@ problem 191 number-of-1-bits
title: Number of 1 Bits
difficulty: Easy
topics: Divide and Conquer, Bit Manipulation
params: n
time: O(number of set bits)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Given a positive integer `n`, return the number of set bits (`1`s) in its binary representation (its **Hamming weight**).

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 2^31 - 1`

=== hints
- `n & (n - 1)` clears the lowest set bit.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int hammingWeight(int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::hammingWeight);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) { return lc::lines(g.irange(1, 2147483647)); });
}

=== approach
`n & (n - 1)` turns off the **lowest set bit** of `n` (subtracting 1 flips that bit and all zeros below it). Repeat until `n` is 0 and count the iterations: the loop runs once per set bit.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while (n) {
            n &= n - 1;     // clear the lowest set bit
            ++count;
        }
        return count;
    }
};

=== examples
11
> 3
: 11 = 1011 in binary.

128
> 1

2147483645
> 30

=== tests
1
> 1

2147483647
> 31

1073741824
> 1

@@@ problem 136 single-number
title: Single Number
difficulty: Easy
topics: Array, Bit Manipulation
params: nums
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1

=== description
Every element of the non-empty array `nums` appears **twice** except for one. Find that single one.

Use linear time and only constant extra space.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^4`
- `-3 * 10^4 <= nums[i] <= 3 * 10^4`
- exactly one element appears once; every other element appears exactly twice

=== hints
- XOR is its own inverse: `x ^ x = 0` and `x ^ 0 = x`, and the order doesn't matter.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::singleNumber);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int pairs = std::max(0, n / 2);
        auto vals = g.distinct(pairs + 1, -30000 - n, 30000 + n);
        std::vector<int> v;
        for (int i = 0; i < pairs; ++i) { v.push_back(vals[i]); v.push_back(vals[i]); }
        v.push_back(vals[pairs]);
        g.shuffle(v);
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
XOR all numbers together. Pairs cancel (`x ^ x = 0`) regardless of order, because XOR is commutative and associative. What remains is the single number (`0 ^ x = x`).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        int x = 0;
        for (int v : nums) x ^= v;   // duplicates cancel out
        return x;
    }
};

=== examples
[2,2,1]
> 1

[4,1,2,1,2]
> 4

[1]
> 1

=== tests
[-1]
> -1

[7,-3,7]
> -3

[0,5,5]
> 0

@@@ problem 137 single-number-ii
title: Single Number II
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Bit Manipulation
params: nums
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1

=== description
Every element of `nums` appears **three times** except for one, which appears exactly once. Find the single one.

Use linear time and only constant extra space.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 3 * 10^4`
- `-2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1`
- one element appears once; every other element appears exactly three times

=== hints
- Count, for each of the 32 bit positions, how many numbers have that bit set. Modulo 3, what's left?
- The two-variable trick (`ones`, `twos`) does the same for all bits at once.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::singleNumber);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int triples = std::max(0, n / 3);
        auto vals = g.distinct(triples + 1, INT_MIN, INT_MAX);
        std::vector<int> v;
        for (int i = 0; i < triples; ++i) for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) v.push_back(vals[i]);
        v.push_back(vals[triples]);
        g.shuffle(v);
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
Think of each bit position separately: over all numbers, the count of set bits at that position is `3 · (something) + (bit of the single number)`. So we need a counter **modulo 3** per bit.

The variables `ones` and `twos` hold such counters for all 32 bits at once: a bit is in `ones` when its count ≡ 1 (mod 3), in `twos` when ≡ 2. For each number `x`:

- `ones = (ones ^ x) & ~twos`
- `twos = (twos ^ x) & ~ones`

These update the counters and reset them to 0 when they reach 3. At the end `ones` holds exactly the bits of the single number.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        unsigned ones = 0, twos = 0;      // per-bit counters mod 3 (state 1 / state 2)
        for (int v : nums) {
            unsigned x = v;
            ones = (ones ^ x) & ~twos;    // 0 -> 1, 1 -> 0 (goes to twos), 2 stays out
            twos = (twos ^ x) & ~ones;    // 1 -> 2, 2 -> 0 (third occurrence)
        }
        return (int)ones;                 // bits that appeared 1 (mod 3) times
    }
};

=== examples
[2,2,3,2]
> 3

[0,1,0,1,0,1,99]
> 99

=== tests
[-7]
> -7

[-2,-2,1,1,4,1,4,4,-4,-2]
> -4

[2147483647,-2147483648,2147483647,2147483647]
> -2147483648

@@@ problem 201 bitwise-and-of-numbers-range
title: Bitwise AND of Numbers Range
difficulty: Medium
topics: Bit Manipulation
params: left, right
time: O(log right)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
Given two integers `left` and `right` describing the range `[left, right]`, return the bitwise AND of **all** numbers in that range, inclusive.

=== constraints
- `0 <= left <= right <= 2^31 - 1`

=== hints
- Any bit that changes somewhere in the range becomes 0 in the result.
- The answer is the **common binary prefix** of `left` and `right`, followed by zeros.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int rangeBitwiseAnd(int left, int right) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::rangeBitwiseAnd);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int) {
        long long a = g.range(0, 2147483647), b = g.coin(0.5) ? a + g.range(0, 64) : g.range(a, 2147483647);
        return lc::lines((int)a, (int)std::min(b, 2147483647LL));
    });
}

=== approach
Counting from `left` to `right`, every bit position below the highest position where `left` and `right` differ flips at some point, so it ends up 0. Bits above that position are the same in every number of the range. So the answer is the **common prefix** of `left` and `right`.

Find it by shifting both right until they are equal (counting the shifts), then shift back left.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int rangeBitwiseAnd(int left, int right) {
        int shift = 0;
        while (left != right) {   // strip differing low bits
            left >>= 1;
            right >>= 1;
            ++shift;
        }
        return left << shift;     // common prefix followed by zeros
    }
};

=== examples
5
7
> 4
: 5 & 6 & 7 = 101 & 110 & 111 = 100.

0
0
> 0

1
2147483647
> 0

=== tests
7
7
> 7

6
7
> 6

2147483646
2147483647
> 2147483646

1073741824
2147483647
> 1073741824
